A||||11||SCCA FIERO||Will the 2.8 bolt to the Isuzu 5 speed?
Z||000000||SCCA FIERO||06-06-99||08:13 PM||||I know it may not handle the power,or will it? I have an Isuzu 5 Speed and a 4speed mounted to my 2.8 now. Not too fond of the 4 speeds ratios so I though maybe I would try this 5 speed. If not will the 4 speed bolt to the 88 cradle? Will it with little mods? Would it be better to find a V6 5 speed since it should bolt in the cradle?
Z||000001||FieroMike||06-07-99||09:08 AM||||I have heard rumours that it is not strong enough, but I and everyone else I know that has one bolted up has no problems.  When I accelerate quickly, or drop the clutch it feels very strong to me.  The 5-speed is so much smoother and less problems, I'd do it.<br>-Mike
Z||000002||Carrolles||06-07-99||11:35 AM||||Visit V8Archie web site.  He talks a lot about how much power the various trasnmissions can take.  He puts v-8s in with the stock transmissions.
Z||000003||SCCA FIERO||06-07-99||02:38 PM||||Fieromike - the 4 cylinder 5 speed is Isuzu and the 5 speed that come in the V6 Fieros is a Getrag/Muncie. My 86SE V6 has a Muncie 4 speed(bullit proof, but notchy and only 4 gears) and my 88 coupe has an Isuzu(smooth, but i dont like ANYTHING that is imported from Japan) and i'm not sure if the Isuzu will bolt to and handle the power of a V6. 
Z||000004||GREENDANO||06-07-99||08:31 PM||||I think the bottom line is YES.  The Isuzu will bolt up.  Can it take the torque?  I guess if you're not big on side-stepping the clutch it will, as per a few well knowledgable Fiero Folks.  I was going to do this swap, (Isuzu in my auto 85 GT) So I have done the research.<br>  Dan-O
Z||000005||mrfiero||06-14-99||05:44 AM||||The Isuzu 5-sp. will bolt right up to the V-6 engines and handle the power with no problems.  The gearing on these is actually better than the Getrag built 5-sps.....so in that respect its great.  The bad news is the shift forks are made out of pot metal and don't take kindly to "power shifting" or any other abuse.  If you drive it carefully and don't get too over zealous in shifting the Isuzu will do just fine on your V-6.<p>Bottom line is they are capable of handing the power of the V-6, but they can't take abusive shifting.  
Z||000006||PaUL||06-14-99||02:23 PM||||If you do this transplant like I did, use the V6 clutch and diaphragm with the throwout bearing for the Isuzu. The v6 clutch has a larger diameter but the splines are the same and will mate with the input shaft. As mentioned the clutch fork is different and will not accept the V6 throwout bearing.  
Z||000007||SCCA FIERO||06-14-99||06:28 PM||||You have done this swap? I have tons of question about it. <p>Will my 2.8 bolt to the Isuzu 5 speed?<p>Will the Isuzu handle the 2.8?<p>I plan to install my 86 gauges will they work with the 5speed?<p>I will have to use my V6 computer - will it work with the Isuzu 5speed?<p>If not and I have to use my 4speed from my 86SE V6 will it mount to the 88 cradle?<br>
Z||000008||mwbackus||06-14-99||06:55 PM||||I would be slightly concerned with the Isuzu behind the V6.  The reason that Pontiac did not offer this trans on the V6 was that "they" did not feel it would handle the increased torque and still be a dependable powertrain.  There may have been other reasons, but it does occur to one that it would have been economically feasible to just use the Isuzu rather than to develop a totally new 5 spd transmission for this application.  Maybe they were on drugs.. :-)
Z||000009||PaUL||06-14-99||08:09 PM||||SCCAFiero, to answer your questions, yes I have done this. The Isuzu trans will bolt up to the 2.8 no problem. You may need to change the plastic gear on the speed sensor to get the correct speed. The correct gear for this trans is red in color, if you have a white or other color then you may need to change it. The only data that the computer gets from the trans is the speed so you should have no problems there. I dont have any experience with the 88s so I couldnt tell you if the muncie will bolt to the 88 cradle but it probably will. <p> 
Z||000010||terryk||08-06-99||12:11 AM||||Here's another way to look at it....<p>The Getrag is about the second most expensive trans to repair next to the Ford AXOD. The 4T80E is not as bad.<p>You can put Isuzu's in all day and not approach the rebuild or junkyard costs of a Getrag.<p>I've beat the living crap out of my 2.8L MPFI/Isuzu'd X-11 and it still works.<p>I love the Getrag, but only if they are free.
Z||000011||SCCA FIERO||08-06-99||12:56 PM||||Well, the Isuzu is stronger than everyone thinks. I have dogged the _hit out of this thing. Hazing the tires all the way through first gear and grabbing second gear and no trouble yet. I do have a Getrag now, but I don't have the brackets that mount the cables and the slave. I plan to drop the cradle over winter and do a few things that need to be done and I may put the Getrag in it(IF SOMEONE WILL HELP ME FIND THOSE BRACKETS :)
